{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "The line that connected Lewiston to the Union Pacific system -- and that deliberately cut Walla Walla out of it. The Snake River Valley Railroad was incorporated in Oregon on March 3 1898 and opened December 1 1899, running 65.85 miles along the left (southeast) bank of the Snake from Wallula to Grange City under OR&N operation. Its stated purpose was to give 'a better route to Spokane than the existing line via Walla Walla': the older OR&N route had gone the long way round through Walla Walla and Starbuck, and this water-level cutoff replaced it. The company also graded part of the right bank above Riparia toward Lewiston, but it was the Oregon, Washington and Idaho Railroad that finished that job and opened Riparia-Lewiston on July 7 1908 (owi_riparia_lewiston.geojson); OR&N already owned the short intermediate stretch from Grange City across the river to Riparia. Both companies were absorbed into the Oregon-Washington Railroad and Navigation Company on December 23 1910. Union Pacific still runs Wallula-Grange City as a main line, now parts of the Ayer and Riparia subdivisions. The chronology is the point: the Walla Walla bypass opened in 1899, NINE YEARS BEFORE Lewiston was connected at all, so Walla Walla and Lewiston were never on a through passenger route together -- see 'passenger_service'.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake_River_Valley_Railroad (incorporated in Oregon March 3 1898; 65.85 miles / 105.98 km on the left/southeast bank of the Snake between Wallula and Grange City, Washington; opened December 1 1899 under OR&N operation; built to provide 'a better route to Spokane than the existing line via Walla Walla'; graded part of the right bank above Riparia toward Lewiston, which the Oregon, Washington and Idaho Railroad instead completed; OR&N already owned the Grange City-Riparia segment; absorbed into the UP's OWR&N subsidiary December 23 1910; UP still operates Wallula-Grange City as main line, now portions of the Ayer and Riparia subdivisions)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WikiProject_Trains/ICC_valuations/Oregon%E2%80%93Washington_Railroad_and_Navigation_Company (ICC valuation: Snake River Valley Railroad Company, 'from Wallula to Grange City, Wash.', 65.85 mi single-track standard gauge, acquired by construction, control date December 23 1910)", "https://www.streamlinerschedules.com/concourse/track12/camasprairie195407.html (July 1954: the Portland-Lewiston 8-Section coach-sleeping car was handled on UP trains 20 and 19 over the Columbia River main line and switched at AYER on this line; Camas Prairie trains 73/74 then worked Ayer-Lewiston. Walla Walla appears nowhere in the schedules)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starbuck,_Washington (Starbuck was 'originally a junction on the main line of the Oregon Railroad and Navigation Company'; OR&N completed a line east from Starbuck to Pomeroy and Pataha City in 1886, in operation until 1981; a Snake River bridge completed downstream from Lyons Ferry in 1914 'greatly diminished the railroad traffic through the town'; shops closed 1932, station closed 1961)"], "passenger_service": "This line DID carry through passenger service -- but past Walla Walla, not through it. As of July 1954 the Portland-Lewiston coach-sleeping car (8-Section) ran on UP trains 20 and 19 along the Columbia River main line and was switched onto the Camas Prairie at Ayer, a point on this line about 30 miles east of Wallula. Walla Walla sat on a branch off Wallula and appears nowhere in the Camas Prairie schedules, so a Walla Walla passenger bound for Lewiston had to change trains. See walla_walla_columbia_river_railroad.geojson and owi_riparia_lewiston.geojson.", "geometry_sources": ["Real-traced from OpenStreetMap (Overpass), shortest rail path Wallula to Riparia over the surviving UP Ayer and Riparia subdivisions. 919 points, 109.89 km / 68.28 mi, with NO bridged gaps -- the entire route matched to connected active track.", "The trace runs Wallula to Riparia (68.28 mi) rather than stopping at Grange City, because Grange City could not be geocoded and the map needs the line to physically meet the Riparia-Lewiston feature. The documented Wallula-Grange City figure is 65.85 mi, and the ~2.4 mi difference is consistent with continuing across the Snake at the Riparia bridge -- but the exact position of Grange City on this trace is NOT confirmed."], "not_yet_sourced": "Frequencies are estimates; only the July 1954 through-car arrangement above is documented, and no timetable for this line's own Portland-Spokane services has been consulted. Also unsourced: running times, the date the pre-1899 OR&N route via Walla Walla and Starbuck was built (one search result dates a Walla Walla-Starbuck line to 1881 but no reliable segment-level source was found) and when it was downgraded, the exact location of Grange City, and the date of the 1914 Lyons Ferry bridge realignment's effect on this specific alignment.", "freight_only_audit": "2026-08-24: removed as part of a repo-wide freight-only audit. Removed here: segment 1958-2026 from snake-river-valley-wallula-riparia ('Freight-only Union Pacific main line'). REPO RULE: this map shows PASSENGER rail only. Freight-only operation is not represented -- a line's segments must end when its passenger service ends. See CONVENTIONS.md.", "line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6."}, "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Snake River Valley Railroad / Oregon-Washington Railroad and Navigation Company (Union Pacific)", "line": "Oregon-Washington Railroad and Navigation: Snake River cutoff", "id": "snake-river-valley-wallula-riparia", "color": "#F2A900", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 60, "speed_source": "estimated: a water-level main line along the Snake with almost no grade; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1899, "end": 1910, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Opened December 1 1899, 65.85 miles Wallula-Grange City on the southeast bank of the Snake, operated by the OR&N. Built explicitly as 'a better route to Spokane than the existing line via Walla Walla' -- this is the cutoff that took Walla Walla off the through route. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake_River_Valley_Railroad", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WikiProject_Trains/ICC_valuations/Oregon%E2%80%93Washington_Railroad_and_Navigation_Company", "https://www.streamlinerschedules.com/concourse/track12/camasprairie195407.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starbuck"]}, {"start": 1910, "end": 1958, "headway_min": 720, "note": "Absorbed into the Union Pacific's Oregon-Washington Railroad and Navigation Company on December 23 1910. From 1908 this line was also the link to Lewiston: the through Portland-Lewiston sleeper was switched onto the Camas Prairie at Ayer, roughly 30 miles east of Wallula. Camas Prairie passenger service ended between July 1957 and November 1958. Frequency estimated, unsourced.", "sources": ["NOT SOURCED AT SEGMENT LEVEL: the references below cover this line, but nothing has been checked specifically against this segment's start year, end year or service. Treat the dates and the headway as unverified. Supporting references for the line:", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snake_River_Valley_Railroad", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WikiProject_Trains/ICC_valuations/Oregon%E2%80%93Washington_Railroad_and_Navigation_Company", "https://www.streamlinerschedules.com/concourse/track12/camasprairie195407.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starbuck"]}]}, "geometry": {"type": "LineString", "coordinates": [[-118.909726, 46.083658], [-118.909991, 46.084311], [-118.910209, 46.084872], [-118.91025, 46.085027], [-118.910783, 46.087022], [-118.91122, 46.08864], [-118.911948, 46.09138], [-118.912303, 46.092671], [-118.912628, 46.093864], [-118.913039, 46.095304], [-118.913453, 46.096644], [-118.913624, 46.097248], [-118.914326, 46.099902], [-118.914628, 46.100996], [-118.914709, 46.101327], [-118.914872, 46.101921], [-118.914891, 46.101978], [-118.915072, 46.102714], [-118.915123, 46.103003], [-118.915147, 46.103304], [-118.915172, 46.103743], [-118.915151, 46.104173], [-118.9151, 46.104589], [-118.914899, 46.106031], [-118.914768, 46.107292], [-118.914768, 46.10735], [-118.914604, 46.10857], [-118.914553, 46.109441], [-118.91462, 46.109874], [-118.914721, 46.110333], [-118.91491, 46.110787], [-118.915188, 46.111317], [-118.915552, 46.111814], [-118.91624, 46.112528], [-118.916852, 46.113008], [-118.918433, 46.114135], [-118.919402, 46.114873], [-118.919729, 46.115194], [-118.92005, 46.115597], [-118.920243, 46.115918], [-118.920432, 46.116328], [-118.920547, 46.116639], [-118.920588, 46.116839], [-118.920588, 46.117035], [-118.920583, 46.11806], [-118.920485, 46.119343], [-118.920477, 46.119476], [-118.920303, 46.121769], [-118.920085, 46.124829], [-118.919078, 46.138245], [-118.919049, 46.138688], [-118.918948, 46.139382], [-118.918851, 46.140088], [-118.918535, 46.141486], [-118.915736, 46.153391], [-118.914588, 46.158321], [-118.913589, 46.162577], [-118.913492, 46.163225], [-118.913491, 46.163674], [-118.913565, 46.164109], [-118.913696, 46.164502], [-118.913901, 46.164875], [-118.914121, 46.165208], [-118.914495, 46.165655], [-11